r lease starting at $5,000 a month\n- 3% increases each year after the first, which brings the rent to about $5,630 in year five\n\nHere's why I think this is fair for both of us:\n\n- **Steady rent from a tenant you know.** I've been here a decade. Apart from two late payments in early 2024, which you kindly forgave, I've paid on time every month.\n- **The space is ready to use.** In 2019 I put about $70,000 into the kitchen, including a new hood system that stays with the building.\n- **The market.** The unit two doors down has been empty for 14 months at $28 a square foot. Your proposed rent works out to about $29 for my space.\n\nI understand a coffee chain has asked about the space. Even so, a long-term tenant who pays on time and stays put means no empty months and no costs to fit the space out for someone new.\n\nI'd be glad to talk this over in person or by phone. I'd be grateful for your answer by June 1 so I have time to plan before the lease ends in August.\n\nThank you for considering this.\n\nSincerely,\n\n[Your name]\nOwner, Pho Lan\n</final_document>\n\nThe letter is about 260 words. It uses \"Dear Mr. Halloran\" because you weren't sure you're on first-name terms. It works as an email or a printed letter.\n\n- **Opening offer:** It opens at $5,000, so you have $100 of room to move up to your $5,100 limit. With 3% raises, your offer reaches about $5,630 by year five, which is almost his asking price. That's a strong point, so the letter states it outright.\n- **Late payments:** You don't know if he's brought them up, so the letter mentions them once, thanks him for waiving the fees, and moves on. That way he can't hold them against you later. If you'd rather leave them out, change that line to \"I've paid on time every month since early 2024.\"\n- **Coffee chain:** The letter doesn't question whether the chain is serious. It reminds him that a new tenant could mean months of empty space and fit-out costs, which a long-term tenant avoids.","ttft_ms":56